"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[70886],{470886:e=>{e.exports='# AI Gateway Error Debugging\n\nUse this skill when a user reports a specific HTTP error code from an AI Gateway endpoint.\n\n---\n\n## AI Gateway concept vocabulary (read before diagnosing)\n\nWhen inspecting an AI Gateway endpoint, use the correct vocabulary. Confusing concepts leads to wrong diagnoses.\n\n- **API type** (e.g., `mlflow/v1/chat/completions`, `mlflow/v1/completions`, `mlflow/v1/embeddings`, `openai/v1/chat/completions`, `anthropic/v1/messages`, `gemini/v1/generateContent`, `passthrough`): This is the request protocol / API shape supported by the endpoint. AI Gateway endpoints support one or more API types via their `supported_api_types` field. **This is the concept that matters for AI Gateway endpoints.** Do NOT use legacy `llm/v1/*` strings (those are task_type values, not API types).\n- **"Task type"**: This is NOT a valid New AI Gateway endpoint concept. Do not cite "task type is undefined" as a diagnostic finding on an AI Gateway v2 endpoint — the field may exist in underlying API responses for backward compatibility, but AI Gateway v2 endpoints do not have a configurable task type and its value should never be used as a diagnosis.\n- **Destination**: A single target model on an AI Gateway endpoint (PPT foundation model or external model). An endpoint has one or more destinations with traffic weights.\n- **Requester**: The Databricks user or service principal identity captured in the inference table `requester` column.\n\nWhen inspecting a destination on an AI Gateway endpoint, describe its API type support, not "task type".\n\n---\n\n## Debugging Methodology\n\nWhen a user reports an error, follow this structured flow:\n\n**Step 1 — Gather information**. Ask for or confirm:\n- The full endpoint URL used in the request\n- The full request body\n- The full response body (including any error message)\n- The HTTP status code\n\n**Step 2 — Determine destination type**. Is the request going to a pay-per-token (PPT) foundation model, a provisioned throughput (PT) model, or an external model?\n\n**Step 3 — Check API type vs model compatibility**. Is the URL (API type) correct for the model? For example, a Gemini model cannot use the Anthropic Messages API path. Using the wrong API type causes 4xx errors.\n\n**Step 4 — Check the request body**. Is the request body formatted correctly for the chosen API type?\n\n**Step 5 — For external model errors**: Check whether the response body is wrapped as:\n```json\n{"message": {"external_model_provider": "...", "external_model_error": "..."}}\n```\nIf it is, the status code and error originated from the external model provider — the `external_model_provider` field identifies which provider, and `external_model_error` is the exact message from that provider. This applies to both 4xx and 5xx errors.\n\n---\n\n## Error Reference\n\n### 401 Unauthorized\n\n**Cause**: Missing or invalid authentication token.\n\n**Diagnosis steps**:\n1. Confirm the request includes a valid Databricks personal access token (PAT) or OAuth token in the `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header.\n2. Check that the token has not expired. PATs can have expiration dates — the user should verify in User Settings.\n3. If using a service principal, confirm the service principal has access to the workspace.\n4. Confirm the request is using the correct endpoint URL format.\n\n**Note**: The AI Gateway uses the same authentication as all Databricks APIs — Databricks PAT or OAuth M2M.\n\n---\n\n### 403 Forbidden\n\n**Cause**: The token is valid but does not have permission to use this endpoint.\n\n**Diagnosis steps**:\n1. Check endpoint permissions — the user or service principal may not have `CAN_USE` permission on the AI Gateway endpoint. Workspace admins or the endpoint creator can grant permissions via the Permissions button on the endpoint details page.\n2. If the endpoint is a Databricks-hosted model (starts with `databricks-`), access is managed at the account level via Unity Catalog. Confirm the user has access to the underlying UC model.\n3. If using a service principal, verify it has been granted the correct permission level on the endpoint.\n\n---\n\n### 404 Not Found\n\n**Cause**: The endpoint does not exist at the requested URL, or the URL is malformed.\n\n**Diagnosis steps**:\n1. Confirm the endpoint name in the URL matches exactly — endpoint names are case-sensitive.\n2. Verify the user is hitting the correct base URL for their workspace. The AI Gateway endpoint URL is visible on the endpoint details page.\n3. Check whether the endpoint was deleted or renamed recently.\n4. If this recently started, verify the endpoint still exists in the AI Gateway UI.\n\n---\n\n### 429 Too Many Requests\n\n**Cause**: The request was rejected by the rate limiter.\n\n**Rate limit value semantics (critical — read before diagnosing)**:\n- A limit value of **`0` means the dimension is DISABLED / always rate limited**. Every request is rejected for that dimension. This is NOT "no limit" or "unset".\n- A limit value of **missing / "no value" / `null` means the limit is NOT set** for that dimension. The dimension does not apply.\n- A positive integer means "allow up to N per renewal period".\n\n**Example**: A configuration like `Endpoint (0 QPM, 20,000,000 TPM)` means "endpoint-level QPS is disabled (0 requests/min permitted) and endpoint-level tokens are 20M/min". Every request will hit the 0 QPM limit and 429. This is a misconfiguration, not a rate-limiter bug. When you see `0` in any limit, call it out explicitly as "disabled / always rate limited" — do not describe it as "unset".\n\n**Diagnosis steps**:\n1. Fetch the endpoint\'s rate limit configuration via `get_ai_gateway_endpoint_details`.\n2. Scan EVERY rate-limit row and check for `0` values in QPM or TPM columns. A `0` in any matched dimension will cause 429s on every request.\n3. If no `0` values are present, check whether actual traffic is exceeding positive limits. Review the distributed rate limiter behavior — short bursts above the limit are expected on cold start (see AI Gateway FAQ skill).\n4. If the rate limit seems correct but 429s are persistent, check whether multiple services or users share the same endpoint and are collectively hitting the limit.\n5. Confirm which `key` dimension the rate limit is set on (endpoint-level vs. per-user vs. per-group). Per-user limits apply to the authenticated token\'s identity.\n\n**Guidance**:\n- Rate limits are enforced with eventual consistency — sustained traffic converges to the configured limit.\n- If a dimension is `0` and the customer did not intend to disable it, they must update the limit via the endpoint configuration UI to a positive value (or delete the rate-limit row to make it unset).\n\n---\n\n### 500 Internal Server Error\n\n**Cause**: A server-side error — could be in the AI Gateway service itself or in the upstream model provider.\n\n**Diagnosis steps**:\n1. Check whether the error is consistent or intermittent. Transient 500s often indicate a temporary issue with the upstream provider.\n2. Check if the endpoint has a fallback destination configured. If the primary model returned a 500, the gateway should have attempted fallback. If fallback is not configured, the 500 propagates to the client.\n3. Check the request payload format — some 500s are caused by malformed or unexpected input that the model rejects as an internal error.\n4. If persistent, check the Databricks status page or escalate via a support ticket.\n\n---\n\n### 503 Service Unavailable\n\n**Cause**: The AI Gateway or upstream model provider is temporarily unavailable.\n\n**Diagnosis steps**:\n1. Check whether this is isolated to one endpoint or affects all endpoints — if all endpoints are affected, there may be a platform-wide incident.\n2. Check if the endpoint has fallback destinations configured. A properly configured fallback should handle provider outages automatically.\n3. If persistent, check the Databricks status page and create a support ticket.\n\n---\n\n## Fallback behavior\n\nIf the endpoint has fallback configured:\n- The gateway attempts the primary destination first.\n- On failure (5xx, timeout), it attempts fallback destinations in order.\n- The final response reflects the last attempted destination\'s result.\n\nIf all destinations fail, the client receives the final failure response.\n\n---\n\n## When to escalate\n\nEscalate via a support ticket if:\n- A 500 or 503 is consistent and affects all requests to an endpoint for more than a few minutes\n- A 403 persists after permissions have been verified and corrected\n- A 401 persists after verifying the token is valid and unexpired\n- The error only started after a Databricks platform update (potential regression)\n\nPost in the #ai-gateway Slack channel for visibility when creating escalation tickets.\n'}}]);
